+# Large Carrier Ownership, Build, Multirole, and Command Benchmark
+
+## Benchmark Question
+
+Can one authoritative carrier system support recognizably different large ships, broad multirole capability, nested mobile bases, legitimate player progression and survivable damage without reducing the carrier to a single-purpose class or a personal inventory item?
+
+# 1. Test Articles
+
+Create six carrier configurations from at least four hull lineages:
+
+1. Hearth profile on a keel-and-spine hull;
+2. Survey profile on the same underlying hull generation;
+3. Relief/refuge profile on the same underlying hull generation;
+4. Ocean interface profile on an ocean-cradle hull;
+5. Security and rescue profile on a deep-hull or shell lineage;
+6. Trade/culture profile on an archipelago or ring lineage.
+
+At least one test must reverse the customary emphasis—for example, a Foundry lineage configured primarily for education and habitation.
+
+# 2. Common-Core Gate
+
+Every configuration must retain effective capacity for:
+
+- habitation;
+- life support;
+- mobility;
+- energy and thermal control;
+- repair and fabrication;
+- care;
+- logistics;
+- information;
+- governance;
+- culture and education;
+- rescue;
+- defense.
+
+A configuration fails if its primary emphasis can only be achieved by silently deleting an ordinary civic function.
+
+# 3. Variety Gate
+
+Blind reviewers should distinguish carriers using more than color or name.
+
+Evaluate:
+
+- silhouette;
+- internal topology;
+- carried-base mix;
+- sounds and motion;
+- workflow;
+- public-space character;
+- failure response;
+- cultural ownership;
+- mission profile.
+
+Target: at least 80% correct family/lineage grouping and at least 70% correct mission-emphasis grouping after names and labels are removed.
+
+# 4. Role-Profile Gate
+
+For each carrier:
+
+- primary-emphasis capacity is measurably stronger;
+- at least four secondary roles remain useful in play;
+- changing the profile alters allocation and workflow;
+- changing the profile does not spawn a disconnected ship class;
+- no single emphasis exceeds the configured concentration limit without explicit emergency or exploitative state.
+
+# 5. Nested Vehicle Gate
+
+Prove the chain:
+
+```text
+civilization carrier
+→ planetary or ocean base-carrier
+→ mobile home
+→ field vehicle
+```
+
+Verify:
+
+- identity persistence;
+- ownership separation;
+- resident persistence;
+- inventories;
+- damage;
+- mass and balance updates;
+- valid docking;
+- aborted docking;
+- deployment;
+- recovery;
+- no duplication after host migration.
+
+# 6. Access and Progression Gate
+
+Playtest four entry relationships:
+
+1. passenger or evacuee;
+2. qualified station operator;
+3. department steward or carried-base captain;
+4. command or construction-program coordinator.
+
+Target findings:
+
+- players experience meaningful carrier play in the first relationship;
+- qualification feels like mastery rather than a level lock;
+- authority scope remains understandable;
+- command creates responsibility and tradeoffs rather than total control;
+- ownership is not required to feel invested.
+
+# 7. Construction Gate
+
+Run one bounded carrier-construction program from requirement to commissioning.
+
+The proof may use compressed time and limited systems, but must include:
+
+- charter;
+- design choice;
+- yard capacity;
+- two supply chains;
+- skilled labor;
+- one quality failure;
+- one financing or political constraint;
+- one systems-integration trial;
+- resident participation;
+- acceptance with recorded defects.
+
+Fail if the dominant experience is manually delivering repeated resource stacks.
+
+# 8. Ownership and Charter Gate
+
+Test at least four ownership models:
+
+- public;
+- cooperative;
+- consortium;
+- private or household.
+
+Verify that title, debt, operational authority, command, resident rights and module ownership can differ.
+
+Fail if a title holder can treat residents or independent carried craft as inventory.
+
+# 9. Single-Player and Co-op Gate
+
+Run the same authoritative emergency scenario in:
+
+- assisted single-player;
+- two-player cooperative;
+- four-player professional operations.
+
+The scenario includes:
+
+- difficult docking;
+- contamination uncertainty;
+- radiator loss;
+- clinic surge;
+- one player or commander disconnect;
+- succession;
+- district isolation;
+- recovery.
+
+Targets:
+
+- all modes remain completable;
+- co-op provides meaningful coordination rather than mandatory seat occupancy;
+- NPC delegation remains competent but not magical;
+- player absence does not freeze the ship.
+
+# 10. Damage and Degradation Gate
+
+The carrier must survive at least three representative failures without becoming fully functional or instantly destroyed.
+
+Required outcomes:
+
+- visible loss of capability;
+- changed route or mission;
+- resource and social consequences;
+- repair plan;
+- possible use of carried craft;
+- preserved authoritative damage state.
+
+# 11. Performance Gate
+
+Representative target hardware should sustain:
+
+- one active carrier locality;
+- one active dock;
+- one deployed base-carrier;
+- named crew near players;
+- aggregated distant districts;
+- deterministic system state;
+- bounded network bandwidth.
+
+Exact budgets remain engine-dependent, but the evidence bundle must report:
+
+- CPU time by simulation layer;
+- memory by carrier graph and active entities;
+- network traffic;
+- save size;
+- loading and locality-transfer time.
+
+# 12. Human Evaluation
+
+After play, ask participants:
+
+- Did the carrier feel like a society or a vehicle level?
+- Could you identify its emphasis without believing it had only one role?
+- Did other people aboard seem to possess agency?
+- Did you understand what you could control?
+- Did construction feel social and industrial?
+- Did damage change the ship rather than merely reduce health?
+- Did the carried mobile home remain emotionally recognizable?
+
+# 13. Kill Criteria
+
+Do not promote if:
+
+- all carriers converge on one optimal build;
+- families differ mainly through paint and labels;
+- specialization deletes civic baseline functions;
+- command behaves as omnipotent ownership;
+- NPCs cannot operate without the player;
+- construction is repetitive inventory delivery;
+- docking duplicates or erases craft;
+- failure is only explosion or full recovery;
+- a single-player assistance mode violates authoritative world rules;
+- players remember the ship's label but not a place or person aboard.
+
+# 14. Evidence Bundle
+
+Required artifacts:
+
+- six carrier profiles;
+- fixed seeds;
+- schema validation;
+- module and district graphs;
+- capacity traces;
+- docking logs;
+- construction history;
+- ownership and authority ledger;
+- damage replay;
+- single/co-op comparison;
+- performance report;
+- participant protocol;
+- blind variety study;
+- known limitations.
+
+## Benchmark Maxim
+
+> **The carrier proof succeeds when six ships can share the same deep rules, perform many of the same necessary functions, and still feel as different as six cities with different histories.**
